Steam Enhanced, client/game-page/layout.js and layout.css: each column of the game page is a stack.

Why. The overview grid placed its cards by auto-placement (`grid-auto-flow: row dense`) on 1px rows,
each card spanning its height. While Steam fills the page (1-2.5 s: friends, cards, the review
block, achievements and the feed come from the network), every card that grows changes the free
places, and dense packing put cards into whichever gap now fits: cards of a column changed place,
and a card that grew stood over the next one for a frame (its span was written in the next animation
frame) before the next one went down. The page "jumped" on each opening.

What. A card's place is fixed by its column and order (layout.css, as before) and nothing else:
fit() lays each column out as a stack, from row 3 (rows 1 and 2: the play bar and the links), each
card at the row where the ones above it end (`--se-row`), spanning its height (`--se-span`). A card
that grows moves only the cards under it, in its own column, by exactly what it grew; none changes
column or order. fit() runs in the ResizeObserver's callback (after layout, before paint: the new
places are in the same frame as the new size) and after a pass adds or removes cards. Column and
order are read from the computed style once per node (getComputedStyle, again only if its section
name changes). A card not measured yet keeps the span it has, or gets none (it is not drawn until
then). A script that knows a page's layout (Arkada's cache of each game's page) can hold rows at
the top of a column for cards Steam mounts late (the cover, the description: ~1 s in): `--se-hold-<column>:
"rows:order"` on the grid; cards ordered before `order` fill the held rows as they come, so the cards
under them do not move. destroy() removes `--se-row` too.

